# The sealed chamber's two biers (TSC_MonSpot_TwoBiers, Insight): the
# pilgrim laid out with honor, and the abbot who laid himself.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_MonTwoBiers

== start ==
The pilgrim was tended: washed, wrapped, laid straight, his warrant folded under his fingers in the place a knight's hands would hold a sword. Whoever did it honored the errand as much as the man.

The abbot had no such hands left to him.

* [Look at the second bier.] -> abbot

== abbot ==
He climbed up alone. The scuff marks are still on the stone lip where an old man's boots fought for purchase, and he arranged himself as best a dying man can: flat, hands folded, habit pulled straight by feel.

Between the two biers, on the floor, sits the space where a wrapped thing rested. He put it where both of them could mind it, and took the last watch of his house.

* [Read the dust.] -> watch

== watch ==
The dust on the floor lies even, unmarked, centuries deep. Nothing crossed this room after him. Nothing disturbed the thing between the biers until the party came down the stairs.

The watch held. Of everything this house was ordered to do, every part of it done at the cost the tallies record, that is the line worth carving somewhere: the watch held.

* [Leave them to it.] -> end
